  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 106 PM EDT Sun Oct 18 2026\n\nEarly this afternoon upper air model height analysis and satellite \nwater vapor imagery showed zonal flow across the northern tier of \nthe United States with multiple closed lows north of the jet. At the \nsurface the Upper Great Lakes was between high pressure over the \neastern seaboard and a strong surface low moving through Ontario, \nwith the U.P. sitting in the warm sector of the system. Little in \nthe way of moisture was available though as warm, dry air in the low \nlevels was advecting east from the central Rockies into the Midwest \nunder strong west-southwest flow. Thus little in the way of \nclouds cover and moisture was accompanying the system's warm\nsector as it moves north of the area. The dry, warm low level \nair combined with gusty winds being mixed to the surface was \nbringing slightly elevated fire weather conditions to much of \nthe U.P. Afternoon temperatures were unseasonably warm, in the \nlow to mid 60s. This is about 10 degrees above average.\n\nTonight the low pressure over Ontario will track north and east of \nthe area dragging a weak cold front south across the U.P. This will \nhelp settle the winds down as high pressure builds overhead through \nMonday. Thereafter, high pressure will dominate the weather pattern \nthis week with light winds, warm afternoons (60s, 10-15 degrees \nabove average), and cool nights with lows in the 30s for inland\nlocations. Humidity will continue to run on the lower side the \nnext few days but elevated fire weather conditions will be \nmitigated by light winds and good humidity recoveries \novernight. The next chance for rain will come late next weekend \nas a stronger cold front sweeps across the area but overall \ntrends are for a relatively dry frontal passage with just a \nchance for showers next weekend.",
